\details{
The Birnbaum-Saunders distribution is the near neighbour of the Wald
(\code{\link[=cogmod_invgaussian]{cogmod_invgaussian()}}), and this family is deliberately parameterized so
that the two can be compared directly: \code{mu} is a drift rate and \code{boundary} a
decision threshold in both, meaning the same thing, so the \emph{only} thing that
differs between them is how the evidence arrives.

\code{ndt} and \code{poutlier} mean exactly what they do in \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}},
and \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} and
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} all work here too. See \code{?rcogmod_lognormal} for why \code{ndt} is
expressed directly in seconds, what the outlier component is for, and why its
scale is a constant rather than a \code{dpar}.
}
\section{Where the distribution comes from}{
A Wald time is the first crossing of a diffusion: evidence moves continuously
and can move \emph{either way} at any instant. Here it instead accumulates in
discrete cycles, and every cycle pushes towards the boundary - what is random
is the \strong{size} of each increment, never its sign. If the increments have
average size \code{mu} and SD 1, then after \code{n} cycles the accumulated evidence is
\code{Normal(n * mu, n)} by the central limit theorem, so

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{P(T <= n) = P(evidence >= boundary) = Phi((mu * n - boundary) / sqrt(n))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

and treating the cycle count \code{n} as continuous turns that into a
first-crossing time. It is one-directional accumulation in discrete chunks,
with a Gaussian approximation standing in for the exact hitting-time
calculation - which is exactly the fatigue-crack process Birnbaum and
Saunders (1969) derived it for.

Fixing the per-cycle SD at 1 is not an arbitrary choice: it is the same
convention that fixes the Wald's diffusion coefficient, and it is what keeps
\code{mu} and \code{boundary} on a common scale across the two families. It also means
there is no third parameter and there cannot be one - the shape is pinned by
\code{mu * boundary}, just as the Wald's shape is pinned at \code{boundary^2}.

The tidy consequence is that

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{(mu * t - boundary) / sqrt(t)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

is \strong{exactly} standard normal. In the distribution's usual \verb{(a, b)}
parameters that transform is written \code{(1 / a) * (sqrt(t / b) - sqrt(b / t))},
with scale \code{b = boundary / mu} and shape \code{a = 1 / sqrt(mu * boundary)}. The
map between the two parameterizations is a bijection -
\code{boundary = sqrt(b) / a} and \code{mu = 1 / (a * sqrt(b))} - so nothing is given
up by stating it mechanistically. Every quantity of the family is elementary
as a result: the CDF is a normal CDF, the quantile function a closed form,
and \code{rcogmod_bisa()} is one normal draw per observation with no rejection
step.
}

\section{Relation to the Wald}{
In these parameters the density is the Wald's own, tilted:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{f_BS(t) = f_Wald(t; mu, boundary) * (mu * t + boundary) / (2 * boundary)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

One sign is all that separates them - the exponent carries
\code{(mu * t - boundary)}, the prefactor \code{(mu * t + boundary)}. The tilt factor
is what makes the Birnbaum-Saunders an \strong{equal mixture of an inverse Gaussian
and a reciprocal inverse Gaussian}: half the mass is the Wald with the same
\code{mu} and \code{boundary}, half is that Wald's length-biased version, which weights
long crossings in proportion to their length.

So at the same \verb{(mu, boundary)} this family is both slower and more spread
out than the Wald. At \verb{mu = 3, boundary = 0.5} its mean is 0.222 s against
the Wald's 0.167, and its SD 0.184 against 0.136. In general

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{E[T]   = boundary / mu   + 1 / (2 * mu^2)    # the Wald's mean, plus a term
Var[T] = boundary / mu^3 + 5 / (4 * mu^4)    # the Wald's variance, plus one
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

both always finite, so \link[brms:posterior_epred.brmsfit]{posterior_epred()} always has
a number to return - unlike \code{\link[=cogmod_invgaussian]{cogmod_invgaussian()}} once its drift varies.
The median is exactly \code{boundary / mu}, which is the Wald's \emph{mean}: do not
read the two families' parameters as describing the same central tendency.

The right tail decays like \code{exp(-mu^2 * t / 2)}, the same exponential order
as the Wald's and much lighter than a LogNormal's, and the density vanishes
at the shift with all its derivatives. \code{ndt} is therefore as well behaved
here as it is for the Wald, with no unbounded-likelihood boundary of the kind
\code{\link[=cogmod_gamma]{cogmod_gamma()}} and \code{\link[=cogmod_weibull]{cogmod_weibull()}} have at shape \verb{< 1}.

There is no \code{sigmadrift}. Across-trial drift variability is what
\code{\link[=cogmod_invgaussian]{cogmod_invgaussian()}} is for; here the extra dispersion comes from the
mixture structure instead, at no cost in parameters.
}

